<h1>Viva Study Guide</h1>
<p class="small">This is your private preparation document. It explains every
concept in the project in plain language, why each design choice was made, and
the questions an examiner is likely to ask &mdash; with answers you can give in
your own words. Read it top to bottom once, then re-read Section&nbsp;6.</p>

<div class="callout"><b>Your 30-second pitch.</b> "Federated learning claims your
data stays private because you only share gradients, not data. I showed that's
false: I built an attack where a curious server reconstructs a client's private
image, pixel by pixel, from just the shared gradient &mdash; at
{{HERO_PSNR}}&nbsp;dB. Then I tested the four privacy defences from our syllabus
&mdash; differential privacy, local DP, compressive privacy, and anonymity &mdash;
against that same attack and measured how much privacy each buys and what accuracy
it costs."</div>

<h2>1&nbsp;&nbsp;The Big Picture</h2>
<p><b>Federated learning (FL).</b> Instead of sending raw data to a server to
train a model, each device keeps its data, computes the <i>gradient</i> of the
model's loss on its local data, and sends only that gradient. The server adds up
everyone's gradients to improve the shared model. Selling point: "your data never
leaves your device."</p>
<p><b>The attack in one line.</b> A gradient is computed <i>from</i> the data, so
it carries information <i>about</i> the data. If you can run the computation
backwards, you get the data back. That backwards step is "gradient inversion."</p>
<p><b>Why it matters.</b> Hospitals, phones, and banks are being sold FL as
"privacy-preserving." If gradients leak the data, that promise is broken &mdash;
which is exactly why the defences in Modules 2&ndash;5 exist.</p>

<h2>2&nbsp;&nbsp;Core Concepts (plain language)</h2>
<h3>2.1&nbsp;&nbsp;What is a gradient, and why does it leak?</h3>
<p>The gradient tells you, for every weight in the network, which direction to
nudge it to reduce the error on the current example. Because the input image
flows through the network to produce that error, the gradient is a detailed,
differentiable fingerprint of the input. The attacker fixes the network weights
and searches for a fake image whose gradient matches the real one &mdash; when the
gradients match, the fake image matches the real image.</p>

<h3>2.2&nbsp;&nbsp;How the attack works (the three tricks)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Cosine matching (not L2).</b> We compare the <i>direction</i> of the two
gradients, not their raw distance. Direction is magnitude-independent, which makes
the attack far more stable. (This is the key idea of Geiping et&nbsp;al. 2020,
"Inverting Gradients".)</li>
<li><b>Total variation (TV).</b> A gentle penalty that keeps neighbouring pixels
similar, so the reconstruction looks like a real image instead of random noise.</li>
<li><b>iDLG label trick.</b> We don't have to guess the label. For a single
example, the true class is the one whose final-layer gradient has a negative sign.
We read it off directly &mdash; 100% accurate for one image.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3&nbsp;&nbsp;How we measure reconstruction quality</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Good value</th></tr>
<tr><td>MSE</td><td>Average squared pixel error</td><td>lower</td></tr>
<tr><td>PSNR (dB)</td><td>Pixel fidelity; &gt;30&nbsp;dB looks almost identical</td><td>higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>SSIM</td><td>Structural similarity (shapes/edges), 0&ndash;1</td><td>higher (&rarr;1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>LPIPS</td><td>Perceptual distance using a neural net; matches human judgement</td><td>lower</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="small">For a <b>defence</b>, you want the opposite: low PSNR/SSIM and high LPIPS mean the reconstruction is bad, i.e. privacy is protected.</p>

<h2 class="sec-break">3&nbsp;&nbsp;The Five Modules &mdash; What Each Means and Where It Appears</h2>

<h3>Module 1 &mdash; Privacy Considerations in ML</h3>
<p>The study of threats and attacks on ML systems. <b>In our project:</b> this is
the attack itself and the <i>threat model</i> &mdash; we assume an
"honest-but-curious" server: it follows the protocol but tries to learn what it
can from what it legitimately receives (the gradient).</p>

<h3>Module 2 &mdash; Differential Privacy (DP)</h3>
<p><b>Idea:</b> add carefully calibrated random noise so that the output barely
changes whether or not any one person's data was included. Formally, a mechanism
is (&epsilon;,&nbsp;&delta;)-DP if including/excluding one record changes output
probabilities by at most a factor of e<sup>&epsilon;</sup>. Small &epsilon; = strong
privacy. <b>DP-SGD</b> applies this to training: clip each example's gradient to a
maximum norm, then add Gaussian noise. <b>In our project:</b> this is the main
defence. Clipping + noise destroys the fine detail the attack needs, and it comes
with a provable guarantee. We sweep the noise level and read off the &epsilon;.</p>

<h3>Module 3 &mdash; Local Differential Privacy (LDP)</h3>
<p><b>Difference from DP:</b> in central DP you trust the server to add noise; in
<b>local</b> DP you trust no one, so <i>each client</i> adds noise to its own data
before sending anything. Stronger privacy model, but more total noise, so more
accuracy is lost. <b>In our project:</b> we apply per-client noise to the gradient
before upload and show it protects more but costs more utility than central DP.</p>

<h3>Module 4 &mdash; Privacy-Preserving Data Mining (k-anonymity)</h3>
<p><b>Idea:</b> before publishing data, make each record indistinguishable from at
least k&minus;1 others (k-anonymity), so no single person stands out. <b>In our
project:</b> federated aggregation is the analogue &mdash; the server sees the
<i>sum</i> of many clients' gradients (secure aggregation), so any one client is
"hidden among k". We analyse why this helps but isn't enough on its own (a
malicious server can still single someone out).</p>

<h3>Module 5 &mdash; Compressive Privacy</h3>
<p><b>Idea:</b> protect privacy by <i>compressing</i> / reducing the dimensionality
of what you share, discarding information an attacker would need while keeping
enough for the task. <b>In our project:</b> two mechanisms &mdash;
<b>gradient pruning</b> (send only the largest values, zero the rest) and
<b>quantization</b> (send fewer bits per value). We sweep how aggressively we
compress and plot privacy vs the "accuracy of the utility task" (the exact phrase
from the syllabus).</p>

<h2>4&nbsp;&nbsp;The Second Attack: Membership Inference (LiRA)</h2>
<p><b>Question it answers:</b> "Was this specific person's record used to train the
model?" Even that yes/no leaks privacy (e.g. "this patient was in the diabetes
study"). <b>LiRA</b> (Carlini 2022) trains many "shadow" models to learn how a
model behaves on data it did/didn't see, then runs a likelihood-ratio test.
<b>Key metric:</b> TPR at a very low FPR &mdash; "can the attacker catch <i>some</i>
members with near-certainty", which is the real-world harm, instead of average
accuracy. <b>Why we include it:</b> it gives a hard number for leakage and lets us
<i>audit</i> differential privacy &mdash; if DP works, this attack should drop to
near chance.</p>

<h2>5&nbsp;&nbsp;Design Decisions (be ready to justify these)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Choice</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>LeNet (small CNN)</td><td>Fits a 4&nbsp;GB GPU; standard in the gradient-leakage literature; reconstructs cleanly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sigmoid, not ReLU</td><td>Smooth activations give smoother gradients, which invert more faithfully.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Untrained network</td><td>Fresh weights leak the most &mdash; the honest worst case, and the clearest demo.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cosine loss</td><td>Direction-based, magnitude-invariant, stable; beats the original L2 method.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Restarts + median</td><td>Reconstruction is seed-sensitive; consensus over restarts stabilises it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CIFAR-10</td><td>Public, non-sensitive, reproducible proxy for private image data &mdash; ethical by design.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 class="sec-break">6&nbsp;&nbsp;Likely Viva Questions &amp; Model Answers</h2>
<h4>Q1. In one sentence, what did you do?</h4>
<p>I showed that federated learning's "only gradients are shared" promise can be
broken by reconstructing private images from gradients, and I measured how well
our syllabus's privacy defences stop that.</p>

<h4>Q2. If no data is sent, how can the server recover the image?</h4>
<p>Because the gradient is computed <i>from</i> the image and the known weights, so
it encodes the image. I fix the weights and optimise a fake image until its
gradient matches the real one &mdash; at which point the fake equals the real.</p>

<h4>Q3. Why cosine similarity and not Euclidean distance?</h4>
<p>Cosine compares direction and ignores magnitude. As a model trains, gradient
magnitudes shrink and vary a lot, which destabilises L2 matching; cosine stays
stable. It's the core improvement of the Inverting-Gradients method.</p>

<h4>Q4. How do you recover the label without the data?</h4>
<p>The iDLG trick: for a single example with cross-entropy, the true class is the
one whose row in the last layer's weight-gradient has negative sign. It's exact
for one image, so I don't optimise the label at all.</p>

<h4>Q5. How does differential privacy stop the attack?</h4>
<p>DP-SGD clips each gradient and adds Gaussian noise, which bounds how much any
one example can affect the gradient. Since the attack feeds on exactly that
per-example signal, the noise blurs the reconstruction &mdash; and it's provable:
you get an (&epsilon;,&nbsp;&delta;) guarantee.</p>

<h4>Q6. Difference between DP and Local DP?</h4>
<p>Central DP trusts the server to add the noise; local DP trusts no one, so each
client adds its own noise before sending. LDP is stronger on trust but needs more
noise overall, so it costs more accuracy. I show both on the same plot.</p>

<h4>Q7. What is the privacy&ndash;utility trade-off?</h4>
<p>More privacy (more noise / more compression) means worse reconstruction for the
attacker but also lower model accuracy. The interesting region is the "knee" where
you block the attack while barely losing accuracy. My sweeps plot exactly this.</p>

<h4>Q8. Why is TPR@low-FPR the right membership-inference metric?</h4>
<p>Average accuracy can look fine while the attack still identifies a few people
with certainty &mdash; and that certainty is the actual harm. TPR at low FPR
measures whether the attacker can confidently catch <i>any</i> member, which is
what privacy should prevent.</p>

<h4>Q9. Where does compressive privacy fit?</h4>
<p>Gradient pruning and quantization both reduce the information in the shared
gradient &mdash; dimensionality/precision reduction. That's compressive privacy:
throw away what the attacker needs while keeping enough for training.</p>

<h4>Q10. Is this a real attack or a simulation?</h4>
<p>It's a real implementation of the attack, run as a single-host simulation of
one federated-learning round with an explicit client/server split. The maths and
code are the genuine attack; the "network" is simulated locally so no real system
or data is touched.</p>

<h4>Q11. What's your individual contribution?</h4>
<p>I built the whole pipeline from scratch and, more importantly, assembled a
single benchmark that pits all four syllabus defence families against one attack
with consistent metrics &mdash; a unified comparison I didn't find pre-existing.</p>

<h4>Q12. Biggest limitation?</h4>
<p>Small model and low-resolution data, and the honest-but-curious assumption.
Absolute numbers would change at scale, and malicious-server attacks are stronger
&mdash; which I discuss and cross-validate against the <i>breaching</i> framework.</p>

<h4>Q13. How would you make the attack stronger?</h4>
<p>Add a BatchNorm network and match BN statistics (GradInversion), use a
generative image prior, or move to a malicious-server analytic attack &mdash; each
is in my references and future-work.</p>

<h4>Q14. How would you make the defence stronger without losing accuracy?</h4>
<p>Combine defences (compress then add DP noise), tune the clipping norm, or use
secure aggregation so the server only ever sees sums &mdash; raising the
anonymity set.</p>

